NNAAMMEE
     ttuunn — tunnel software network interface

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following line in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ddeevviiccee ttuunnttaapp

     Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
     following lines in loader.conf(5):

           if_tuntap_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttuunn interface is a software loopback mechanism that can be loosely
     described as the network interface analog of the pty(4), that is, ttuunn
     does for network interfaces what the pty(4) driver does for terminals.

     The ttuunn driver, like the pty(4) driver, provides two interfaces: an in‐
     terface like the usual facility it is simulating (a network interface in
     the case of ttuunn, or a terminal for pty(4)), and a character-special de‐
     vice “control” interface.  A client program transfers IP (by default)
     packets to or from the ttuunn “control” interface.  The tap(4) interface
     provides similar functionality at the Ethernet layer: a client will
     transfer Ethernet frames to or from a tap(4) “control” interface.

     The network interfaces are named “tun0”, “tun1”, etc., one for each con‐
     trol device that has been opened.  These network interfaces persist until
     the _i_f___t_u_n_t_a_p_._k_o module is unloaded, or until removed with the
     ifconfig(8) command.

     ttuunn devices are created using interface cloning.  This is done using the
     “ifconfig tunNN create” command.  This is the preferred method of creating
     ttuunn devices.  The same method allows removal of interfaces.  For this,
     use the “ifconfig tunNN destroy” command.

